What is the most common cause of hypoparathyroidism?
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
The most common cause of hypoparathyroidism is surgical resection of the parathyroid glands, typically occurring as a complication of thyroidectomy. This leads to hypocalcemia, which manifests as increased neuromuscular excitability. Symptoms range from mild tingling in the hands and feet to severe muscle cramps, laryngeal stridor, and convulsions. Neuropsychiatric manifestations, such as depression and psychosis, may also occur. Approximately 1% of patients undergoing surgery for primary hyperparathyroidism develop irreversible hypoparathyroidism.  

