How does chronic stress affect the functionality of natural killer cells in the immune system and what is the underlying mechanism responsible for this effect?
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
Chronic stress has been shown to negatively affect the functionality of natural killer (NK) cells in the immune system. Natural killer cells are a type of white blood cell that plays a crucial role in the body's defense against viruses and cancer cells. They are part of the innate immune system and can recognize and eliminate infected or malignant cells without prior exposure or sensitization.

The underlying mechanism responsible for the effect of chronic stress on NK cell functionality involves the activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis and the sympathetic nervous system (SNS). These two systems are the primary mediators of the stress response in the body.

When an individual experiences chronic stress, the HPA axis is continuously activated, leading to the release of stress hormones such as cortisol. Cortisol has immunosuppressive effects, which can impair the function of immune cells, including NK cells. High levels of cortisol can reduce the production of cytokines, which are signaling molecules that help regulate the immune response. This can result in a decreased ability of NK cells to recognize and eliminate target cells.

Additionally, the activation of the SNS during chronic stress leads to the release of catecholamines, such as adrenaline and noradrenaline. These neurotransmitters can also have immunosuppressive effects on NK cells. Catecholamines can bind to specific receptors on the surface of NK cells, leading to changes in their function and a reduced ability to kill target cells.

Furthermore, chronic stress can also affect the distribution of NK cells within the body. Studies have shown that stress can cause a redistribution of NK cells from the blood to other tissues, such as the spleen and bone marrow. This can result in a reduced number of circulating NK cells, which may impair the overall immune response.

In summary, chronic stress can negatively impact the functionality of natural killer cells in the immune system through the activation of the HPA axis and the SNS. This leads to the release of stress hormones and neurotransmitters that have immunosuppressive effects on NK cells, impairing their ability to recognize and eliminate target cells. Additionally, chronic stress can affect the distribution of NK cells within the body, further compromising the immune response.
