\[
\text{Jogger speed} = 9 \text{ km/hr} = 9 \times \frac{1000}{3600} \text{ m/s} = 25 \text{ m/s}
\]

The train's speed is 45 km/hr. To convert this to m/s, we multiply by 1000 and then by 1/3600:
\[
\text{Train speed} = 45 \text{ km/hr} = 45 \times \frac{1000}{3600} \text{ m/s} = 12.5 \text{ m/s}
\]

The train is moving at 12.5 m/s and the jogger is moving at 25 m/s, so the relative speed of the train relative to the jogger is the difference between their speeds since they are moving in the same direction:
\[
\text{Relative speed} = 12.5 \text{ m/s} - 25 \text{ m/s} = -12.5 \text{ m/s}
\]
However, since the train is moving at a higher speed, we take the difference as:
\[
\text{Relative speed} = 45 \text{ km/hr} - 9 \text{ km/hr} = 36 \text{ km/hr}
\]
Converting this to m/s:
\[
\text{Relative speed} = 36 \times \frac{1000}{3600} \text{ m/s} = 10 \text{ m/s}
\]

