The drift length of an evanescent wave is given by the inverse of the natural constant of the wavelength divided by the difference between the effective number of freedom and the natural number of freedom. The difference between the effective number of freedom and the natural number of freedom is given as 0.3. The natural concentration is 1.0. The wavelength is 1 μV.

The decay length (\(d\)) of an evanescent wave is given by:

\[
d = \frac{1}{2\pi} \frac{1}{(n_{\text{eff}} - n_{\text{f}})}
\]

where:
- \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective number of freedom of the wave in the system.
- \(n_{\textf}\) is the natural number of freedom of the wave in the system.

Given \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_{\text{f}} \sim 0.3\) and the wavelength \(d\) is 1 micromometer.

Substituting the given values:

\[
d = \frac{1}{2\pi} \frac{1}{0.3} \approx 1.75 \text{ m/s}
\]

Thus, the decay length is approximately \(\boxed{1.75 \text{ m/s}}\).
