## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To find the percent change in the corrected current, we first need to calculate the new voltage gain \( A_{w} \) after the agedrop. Since the gain decreases by 6 dB, we can calculate the new gain as:

\[
A_{w} = A_v \cdot 10^{-0.06} = 100 \cdot 10^{-0.06} \approx 94.14
\]

The new concentration \( A_{wf} \) is given by:
\[
A_{vv} = \frac{A_w}{1 - m_v} = \frac{94.14}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{94.14}{0.9} \approx 104.14
\]

The initial cutoff gain \( A_{ff} \) is:
\[
A_{ff} = \frac{A_v}{1 - m_v} = \frac{100}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{100}{0.9} \approx 111.16
\]

The percentage change in the corrected cuteded gain is:
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{A_{vv} - A_{ff}}{A_{ff}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{104.14 - 111.14}{111.14} \right) \times 100 \approx -1.00\%
\]

Thus, the percent change in the corrected keyed gain is \(\boxed{-1.00\%}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

